  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>BNEsim</strong> if you travel to the same country on an irregular schedule and want data that's already discounted at checkout, no code hunting required. Pick <strong>UPeSIM</strong> if you want a cheaper sticker price backed by a working discount code and you're fine with your data simply expiring if you don't use it. Neither provider has figured out a working referral code for us to show you here, so this one comes down to how each handles pricing and expiry, not which code is bigger.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Automatic Sale Price vs. a Stacking Code</h2>
  <p>
    BNEsim doesn't run a discount-code system at all. Its own pricing pages show up to 70% off as a struck-through original price next to a lower sale price, applied automatically before you type anything in. UPeSIM runs the opposite model: its sticker price is the real price, and getting it down to the actual cost requires one of three working 10% codes (REDDIT10, ESIMDB10, or T-DEALZ-10) at checkout. If you forget the code, you pay full UPeSIM price; BNEsim's discount is already baked in whether you remember a code or not.
  </p>
  <p>
    That changes how you should actually compare the two: don't just hold the raw sticker prices side by side. Compare BNEsim's already-discounted sale price against UPeSIM's price after one of its codes is applied, since that's the number each traveler actually pays.
  </p>

  <h2>Data Expiry: Pay More to Never Expire, or Pay Less and Watch the Clock</h2>
  <p>
    This is the real decision point between these two. BNEsim sells every data pack twice: a cheaper version that expires in 30 days, and a pricier version of the exact same pack with no expiration at all. UPeSIM sells one version only, a fixed block of days at a flat price, no auto-renewal and no option to pay extra for it to stick around longer. Buy a UPeSIM block and don't use it before the window closes, and it's gone; buy BNEsim's no-expiration tier and it sits ready for whenever you land, however long that takes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both also let you buy ahead of a trip without burning the clock early, but the mechanics differ. BNEsim lets you schedule a plan to start activating on a future date you pick. UPeSIM instead waits for your phone to actually register on the destination network before the countdown starts, but its own FAQ warns that an eSIM profile installed and never activated goes stale and shows as expired after 60 days regardless. If you're buying more than two months out, BNEsim's explicit scheduling is the safer bet of the two.
  </p>

