  <h2>Is BNEsim or UPeSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with BNEsim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country occasionally rather than on a set schedule, and want data that doesn't expire while you wait</li>
        <li>You'd rather pay with crypto or run a top-up balance than check out fresh every trip</li>
        <li>You want to lock in a specific future activation date rather than rely on network detection</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with UPeSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the lower sticker price and don't mind applying a code to get it</li>
        <li>You're installing the eSIM within about two months of departure, where its stale-after-60-days limit isn't a problem</li>
        <li>You'd rather a plan simply expire on a fixed schedule than pay a premium for one that doesn't</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither of these fits everyone. If you want the lowest raw per-GB price with a working code and don't care about no-expiration tiers or sale pricing, check <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a> instead; both run cheaper straight per-GB pricing with a code that applies at checkout. For eSIM device compatibility, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>BNEsim vs UPeSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, BNEsim or UPeSIM</h3>
      <p>It depends on the pack. Compare BNEsim's already-discounted sale price against UPeSIM's price after applying REDDIT10, ESIMDB10, or T-DEALZ-10, since those are the real numbers each traveler pays. Neither sticker price alone tells the full story.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either provider's data expire</h3>
      <p>UPeSIM's always does, on a fixed schedule with no auto-renewal. BNEsim sells both a cheaper 30-day version and a pricier no-expiration version of the same pack, so you can choose which tradeoff you want.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I buy either one well ahead of my trip</h3>
      <p>Yes, but they handle it differently. BNEsim lets you schedule a specific future activation date. UPeSIM waits for network connection to start the clock, but its own FAQ warns an installed-and-never-activated profile goes stale after 60 days, so it's the riskier choice for a trip still months away.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a discount code</h3>
      <p>UPeSIM does: REDDIT10 and two backups all give 10% off. BNEsim doesn't run codes at all; its discounts of up to 70% show up automatically as a struck-through sale price instead.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

